To render your app in dark mode during tests, you can use the `colorScheme` option. There are several ways to apply this depending on the scope you need:

### Global or Project-level Configuration
To apply dark mode to all tests or a specific project, set the `colorScheme` property within the `use` object in your `playwright.config.ts` file:

```typescript
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  use: {
    colorScheme: 'dark',
  },
});
```

### Per-file or Per-group Configuration
To apply dark mode to a specific test file or a `test.describe` block, use `test.use()`:

```typescript
import { test } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe(() => {
  test.use({ colorScheme: 'dark' });

  test('my test with dark mode', async ({ page }) => {
    // ...
  });
});
```

### Per-test Configuration
To apply dark mode to an individual test, use `test.use()` within the test file:

```typescript
import { test } from '@playwright/test';

test.use({ colorScheme: 'dark' });

test('my test with dark mode', async ({ page }) => {
  // ...
});
```

### Runtime Emulation
If you need to change the color scheme dynamically during a test, you can use `page.emulateMedia()`:

```typescript
await page.emulateMedia({ colorScheme: 'dark' });
```

Additionally, when creating a new browser context or page, you can specify the color scheme in the options:

```typescript
// Create context with dark mode
const context = await browser.newContext({ colorScheme: 'dark' });

// Create page with dark mode
const page = await browser.newPage({ colorScheme: 'dark' });
```

