Yes, you can speed up your tests by preventing images from loading. You can achieve this by using `page.route()` to abort requests for image files.

According to the documentation, you can use a pattern to match image file extensions and abort those requests. You can apply this at the page level or the context level:

**At the page level:**
```javascript
const page = await browser.newPage();
await page.route('**/*.{png,jpg,jpeg}', route => route.abort());
await page.goto('https://example.com');
```

**At the context level:**
```javascript
const context = await browser.newContext();
await context.route('**/*.{png,jpg,jpeg}', route => route.abort());
const page = await context.newPage();
await page.goto('https://example.com');
```

Alternatively, you can use a regex pattern to achieve the same result:
```javascript
await page.route(/(\.png$)|(\.jpg$)/, route => route.abort());
```

