llm_response:
To ensure your test does not call a real backend and instead returns fixed data, you should use the `page.route` method to intercept the API request and `route.fulfill` to provide the static response.

### Recommended Approach
Use `page.route` to match the API endpoint and `route.fulfill` to return your fixed data. This prevents any actual network requests to the backend.

**Example:**
```javascript
test("mocks an api call and doesn't call the backend", async ({ page }) => {
  // Mock the API call before navigating
  await page.route('*/**/api/v1/fruits', async route => {
    const json = [{ name: 'Strawberry', id: 21 }];
    await route.fulfill({ json });
  });

  // Go to the page; the API call will be intercepted and return the mock data
  await page.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking');

  // Assert that the mock data is present
  await expect(page.getByText('Strawberry')).toBeVisible();
});
```

### Alternative Approach
If you have recorded your API interactions into a HAR file, you can use `page.routeFromHAR` to replay those responses. This also prevents the test from hitting the real API.

**Example:**
```javascript
test('gets the json from HAR', async ({ page }) => {
  // Replay API requests from HAR. 
  // Set update to false to ensure it only uses the HAR file.
  await page.routeFromHAR('./hars/fruit.har', {
    url: '*/**/api/v1/fruits',
    update: false,
  });

  await page.goto('https://demo.playwright.dev/api-mocking');
  // ... assertions
});
```

Both methods ensure the API is never actually called by fulfilling the request with your provided data.

